                    %mi R
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_B(X)[-1] \to R\Gamma(X, A(i))_D) \to R\Gamma(X, F^i) \oplus R\Gamma(X, A(i)) \to.
            %p
              One checks that it is compatible with the cup product. Since the morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi D
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A(i)_D \to A(i)
              sends first
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern+classes"} Chern classes
              in Deligne cohomology to first Chern classes in
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Betti+cohomology"} Betti cohomology
              = succeed "s" do
                %span.newWikiWord
                  projective bundle formula
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/projective+bundle+formula"} ?
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Betti+cohomology"} Betti cohomology
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+cohomology"} de Rham cohomology
              show that the map in question induces an isomorphism on the leftmost and rightmost members of the triangle. Hence the result follows.
          %p
